Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedtest.shoddy — test program for the thirteen reckoner seeds
Rem
Rem Assert-driven, in the cuttletest.shoddy / reckonertest.shoddy pattern:
Rem it prints what it is doing and stops the run at the first assertion
Rem that fails, ending with ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED when it does not.
Rem
Rem cuttletest.shoddy and reckonertest.shoddy already cover the engine's
Rem own contract (T1-T8, T10-T14, T18, and Appendix B.1-B.3/B.6/B.7)
Rem against the engine core plus a mini test seed. What is left, and what
Rem this file is for, is everything that only shows up once the REAL
Rem seeds are in the dictionary: a guard written into an actual seed word
Rem (T9's seed rows), abort-safety across real I/O — files, CSV, isam
Rem (T15), the promise that no handle ever reaches the stack (T16), the
Rem worked data session (T17, and Appendix A.3 entered exactly as the
Rem appendix writes it), and B.5's converting bridge. Every guard below is
Rem walked exactly once, per the test approach's "the guard list is the
Rem test list."
Rem
Rem SURVIVAL IS PROVED BY THE NEXT LINE. Every abort-safety assertion
Rem below is in two parts: the failing line answers a refusal (or, for
Rem the words that are guarded by construction, an honest answer) with
Rem the state intact, AND a further line evaluates afterwards — "the mill
Rem did not die" is the actual contract, and only a subsequent line
Rem proves it.
Rem
Rem WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: T15.3, "READLINES on an unreadable
Rem existing file." TryReadFileTests.cs (phase 3's own C# suite, with
Rem full runtime access) tests only "missing" and "is a directory" for
Rem the same reason this file does not add a third: forcing a genuine
Rem UnauthorizedAccessException from a committed, offline, cross-platform
Rem fixture is not reliable — permission bits do not survive a Windows
Rem checkout the way a file's bytes do, and no builtin this seed can
Rem reach opens a file with a share mode that would conflict with
Rem TryReadFile's own read. The guard exists in the runtime (Engine.cs's
Rem ReadWhy names ACCESS DENIED explicitly); it is simply not exercised
Rem from here, on the same ground the phase-3 suite already established.
Rem
Rem Fixtures: tst/csv/sales.csv (250 generated rows, Appendix A.3 and
Rem T17), tst/csv/malformed.csv (an unterminated quoted cell, T15.6).
Rem Both committed beside this file, per the test approach.
Rem
Rem Build (once): build.ps1 machines
Rem Run:          bin\mill run tst\seedtest.shoddy      (from the repo root)
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../machines/cuttle.shoddy"
Include "../machines/reckoner.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seq.shoddy"
Include "../machines/str.shoddy"
Include "../machines/csv.shoddy"
Include "../machines/stats.shoddy"
Include "../machines/isam.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedmath.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedstats.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedseq.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedstr.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedmoney.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedmatrix.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seeddict.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedfile.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedcsv.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedisam.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedclock.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedrandom.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seeds/seedbool.shoddy"

Rem ---- reading an answer --------------------------------------------------
Rem The same shapes reckonertest.shoddy reads with, repeated here rather
Rem than shared, in the house pattern of each test file standing alone.

Def Ran(st As RckState, line As String) As RckState
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckNext(st2, out)
            st2
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            st2
        Case Else
            st

Def Runs(st As RckState, lines As List Of String) As RckState
    Fold(lines, st, Ran)

Def Bad(st As RckState, line As String) As Boolean
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Def Why(st As RckState, line As String) As String
    Select Case RckEval(st, line)
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            why
        Case Else
            ""

Def Text(st As RckState) As String
    Join(Map(CutCells(RckStack(st)), Fn(c) => CutShow(c, RckFmt(st))), " | ")

Def Deep(st As RckState) As Number
    CutDepth(RckStack(st))

Def TopNum(st As RckState) As Number
    If Deep(st) < 1 Then
        0 - 999999
    Else
        NumOf(First(CutCells(RckStack(st))))

Def NumOf(c As Cell) As Number
    Select Case c
        Case CutNum(x)
            x
        Case Else
            0 - 999999

Rem Trig, statistics and anything transcendental compare inside this;
Rem only results exact by construction compare with =.
Def Tol() As Number
    0.0001

Def Near(a As Number, b As Number) As Boolean
    Abs(a - b) <= Tol()

Def Q(s As String) As String
    Chr(34) & s & Chr(34)

Rem The two-part abort-safety assertion: the line answers for the stated
Rem reason with the state as it was, AND a further line evaluates
Rem afterwards. Only the second half proves the mill lived.
Def Refuses(st As RckState, line As String, why As String) As Boolean
    Bad(st, line) And Instr(Why(st, line), why) > 0 And Text(Ran(st, line)) = Text(st) And Text(Ran(Ran(st, line), "1 1 +")) = Text(Ran(st, "1 1 +"))

Rem The abort-safety shape T15 and T17's ISAMGETOR need: not every
Rem guarded word REFUSES on the bad case (WRITELINES answers False,
Rem ISAMGETOR answers the default), so survival there is just "a further
Rem line still evaluates" without insisting on a particular refusal or on
Rem the stack being unchanged, since the line under test may have pushed
Rem an honest answer rather than refusing at all.
Def Survives(st As RckState, line As String) As Boolean
    Not Bad(Ran(st, line), "1 1 +")

Rem ---- an isam fixture, opened once, seeded through as an ALREADY-OPEN
Rem handle (R3.5(b)) -----------------------------------------------------
Rem The record shape isamtest.shoddy's own fixture uses, in miniature: a
Rem NUMBER key and a short STRING value.

Type Kv
    K As Number
    V As String

Def RdKv(f As Number) As Kv
    Kv(GetNum(f), GetStr(f, 20))

Def WrKv(f As Number, r As Kv)
    PutNum(f, K(r))
    PutStr(f, V(r), 20)

Def KvKey(r As Kv) As Number
    K(r)

Def KvCell(r As Kv) As Cell
    CutPair(Pair(CutNum(K(r)), CutStr(V(r))))

Def IsamPath() As String
    "tst/csv/_seedtest_isam.tmp"

Def IsamWipe()
    If FileExists(IsamPath()) Then
        DeleteFile(IsamPath())
    If FileExists(IsamPath() & ".idx") Then
        DeleteFile(IsamPath() & ".idx")

Def FreshIsam() As Isam
    IsamWipe()
    Let db0 = IsamOpen(IsamPath(), 4, RdKv, WrKv, KvKey)
    Let db1 = IsamInsert(db0, Kv(1, "ONE"))
    Let db2 = IsamInsert(db1, Kv(2, "TWO"))
    IsamInsert(db2, Kv(3, "THREE"))

Rem ---- the whole seeded dictionary --------------------------------------
Rem All thirteen seeds, folded over the engine core, exactly the shape a
Rem mill that wants every domain would build. This is also R2.4's own
Rem test, walked at scale: thirteen domains arrived with zero engine
Rem changes.

Def AllSeeded(db As Isam) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckSeedMath(RckNew())
    Let s2 = RckSeedStats(s1)
    Let s3 = RckSeedSeq(s2)
    Let s4 = RckSeedStr(s3)
    Let s5 = RckSeedMoney(s4)
    Let s6 = RckSeedMatrix(s5)
    Let s7 = RckSeedDict(s6)
    Let s8 = RckSeedFile(s7)
    Let s9 = RckSeedCsv(s8)
    Let s10 = RckSeedIsamNum(s9, db, KvCell)
    Let s11 = RckSeedClock(s10)
    Let s12 = RckSeedRandom(s11)
    RckSeedBool(s12)

Rem ---- reading the CSV fixture directly, for T17.3's "agree with
Rem stats.shoddy called directly" -----------------------------------------

Def SalesPath() As String
    "tst/csv/sales.csv"

Def SalesAmounts() As List Of Number
    Let sh = CsvSheetLoad(SalesPath())
    Map(CsvBody(sh), Fn(r) => CsvNumOr(sh, r, "Amount", 0))

Def Main()
    Let db = FreshIsam()
    Let s0 = AllSeeded(db)

    Print("--- thirteen seeds, zero engine changes, one dictionary ---")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "LOGB"), "SEED-MATH IS IN THE DICTIONARY")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "MEAN"), "SEED-STATS TOO")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "SORTL"), "SEED-SEQ")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "SPLIT"), "SEED-STR")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "MSPLIT"), "SEED-MONEY")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "MMUL"), "SEED-MATRIX")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "DPUT"), "SEED-DICT")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "READLINES"), "SEED-FILE")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "CSVLOAD"), "SEED-CSV")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "ISAMALL"), "SEED-ISAM")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "STAMP"), "SEED-CLOCK")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "RANDOM"), "SEED-RANDOM")
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "BITAND"), "SEED-BOOL")
    Rem Not bridged, per R4.7: the mode words are the only spelling, so
    Rem RAD is still the engine core's, not something seed-math redefined.
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "RAD"), "RAD IS STILL A WORD")
    Assert(SeedOf(s0, "RAD") = "core", "AND IT IS STILL THE ENGINE CORE'S, NOT SEED-MATH'S")

    Print("--- T10, walked over the real dictionary, not a mini-seed ---")
    Assert(Length(RckDict(s0)) > 110, "THIRTEEN SEEDS PLUS THE CORE IS WELL PAST A HUNDRED AND TEN WORDS")
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => WordEffect(e) <> ""), "NO WORD HAS AN EMPTY STACK-EFFECT STRING")
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => WordDesc(e) <> ""), "NO WORD HAS AN EMPTY DESCRIPTION")
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => WordSeed(e) <> ""), "AND EVERY WORD KNOWS ITS SEED")
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => Length(RckHelp(s0, WordName(e))) = 3), "AND HELP ANSWERS FOR EVERY ONE OF THEM")
    Assert(Holds(RckWords(s0), "-- seed-isam --"), "WORDS GROUPS BY SEED, INCLUDING ISAM")
    Assert(Holds(RckHelp(s0, "READLINES"), "touches the world"), "AN EFFECTFUL SEED WORD SAYS SO")
    Assert(Holds(RckHelp(s0, "MEAN"), "touches nothing"), "AND A PURE ONE SAYS SO TOO")

    Print("--- seed-math: LOGB, LOG2, REMAP, ROUNDTO ---")
    Let g = Ran(s0, "1 2")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "2 0 LOGB", "LOGB has no answer for x at or below zero"), "LOGB OF X AT OR BELOW ZERO")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1 8 LOGB", "LOGB needs a base greater than zero and not equal to one"), "LOGB TO BASE ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "-1 8 LOGB", "LOGB needs a base greater than zero and not equal to one"), "LOGB TO A NEGATIVE BASE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "0 LOG2", "LOG2 has no answer at or below zero"), "LOG2 OF ZERO")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "-1 LOG2", "LOG2 has no answer at or below zero"), "AND OF A NEGATIVE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "5 1 1 10 20 REMAP", "REMAP needs an input range with two different ends"), "REMAP WITH A ZERO-WIDTH INPUT RANGE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1.5 16 ROUNDTO", "ROUNDTO needs a place count no wider than 15"), "ROUNDTO PAST FIFTEEN PLACES")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "2 8 LOGB")) = "3", "AND LOGB STILL WORKS")
    Assert(Near(TopNum(Ran(s0, "0 0 3 4 DIST")), 5), "AND SO DOES DIST")

    Print("--- seed-stats: every guard stats.shoddy would otherwise abort on ---")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ } MEAN", "MEAN needs at least one value, the list is empty"), "MEAN OF AN EMPTY LIST")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ } MEDIAN", "MEDIAN needs at least one value, the list is empty"), "MEDIAN OF AN EMPTY LIST")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ } STDDEVP", "STDDEVP needs at least one value, the list is empty"), "STDDEVP OF AN EMPTY LIST")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 } STDDEV", "STDDEV needs at least two values, got 1"), "STDDEV OF ONE VALUE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 } VAR", "VAR needs at least two values, got 1"), "VAR OF ONE VALUE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "{ } SUM")) = "0", "AND SUM OF NOTHING IS ZERO, NOT A REFUSAL")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ } 0.5 QUANTILE", "QUANTILE needs at least one value, the list is empty"), "QUANTILE OF AN EMPTY LIST")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 2 3 } 1.5 QUANTILE", "QUANTILE needs p between 0 and 1, got 1.5"), "QUANTILE PAST ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 2 3 } -0.1 QUANTILE", "QUANTILE needs p between 0 and 1, got -0.1"), "AND BELOW ZERO")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 2 } { 1 2 3 } CORREL", "CORREL needs two lists of the same length, got 2 and 3"), "CORREL OF LISTS OF DIFFERENT LENGTHS")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 } { 2 } CORREL", "CORREL needs at least two values in each list, got 1"), "CORREL OF TOO SHORT A LIST")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 1 1 } { 1 2 3 } CORREL", "CORREL needs both lists to vary, and one of them does not"), "CORREL WHEN ONE SIDE IS CONSTANT")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "0 NORMINV", "NORMINV needs p strictly between 0 and 1"), "NORMINV AT ZERO")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1 NORMINV", "NORMINV needs p strictly between 0 and 1"), "AND AT ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1 0 TCDF", "TCDF needs a positive degrees of freedom"), "TCDF WITH NO DEGREES OF FREEDOM")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1 -5 TCDF", "TCDF needs a positive degrees of freedom"), "AND A NEGATIVE COUNT")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "{ 2 4 4 4 5 5 7 9 } STDDEV")) <> "", "AND STDDEV STILL WORKS ON A REAL LIST")
    Rem The dictionary word is the machine's own name, uppercased, and
    Rem nothing else: stats.shoddy declares StdDev and StdDevP, so a
    Rem session that knows the machine can find the word without a
    Rem translation table. The one-D spelling is gone rather than aliased,
    Rem because two spellings of one idea is what seedbuiltin's own rules
    Rem exist to prevent.
    Assert(RckKnows(s0, "STDDEV") And RckKnows(s0, "STDDEVP"), "STANDARD DEVIATION IS SPELLED THE WAY STATS.SHODDY SPELLS IT")
    Assert(Not RckKnows(s0, "STDEV") And Not RckKnows(s0, "STDEVP"), "AND THE ONE-D SPELLING IS NOT A SECOND WAY IN")

    Print("--- seed-matrix: converting at the bridge, and every pre-flight ---")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "2 2 { 1 2 3 } MAT", "MAT needs 4 numbers for a 2x2 matrix, got 3"), "MAT WITH THE WRONG COUNT OF NUMBERS")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "0 2 { } MAT", "MAT needs r and c of 1 or more, got 0 and 2"), "MAT WITH A ROW COUNT OF ZERO")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "2 2.5 { } MAT", "MAT needs a whole number of 0 or more, got 2.5"), "MAT WITH A FRACTIONAL COLUMN COUNT")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "0 IDENT", "IDENT needs a whole number of 1 or more, got 0"), "IDENT OF SIZE ZERO")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "2 IDENT 3 IDENT MATADD", "+ needs two matrices of one shape, got 2x2 and 3x3"), "MATADD OF DIFFERENT SHAPES")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "2 3 { 1 2 3 4 5 6 } MAT 2 IDENT MMUL", "* needs the columns of the first to match the rows of the second, got 2x3 and 2x2"), "MMUL WHERE THE INNER DIMENSIONS DO NOT MATCH")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "2 IDENT 3 1 MGET", "MGET needs row 1 to 2 and column 1 to 2, got 3 and 1"), "MGET PAST THE LAST ROW")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "2 IDENT 1 0 MGET", "MGET needs row 1 to 2 and column 1 to 2, got 1 and 0"), "AND BEFORE THE FIRST COLUMN")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 2 } { 1 2 3 } DOT", "DOT needs two lists of the same length, got 2 and 3"), "DOT OF LISTS OF DIFFERENT LENGTHS")
    Rem B.5: every vector-taking word here is a LIST in, a LIST or a
    Rem NUMBER out — no ARRAY cell is ever built at the keyboard (R4.16).
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "{ 1 2 } { 3 4 } DOT")) = "11", "DOT TAKES TWO LISTS AND ANSWERS A NUMBER")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "2 3 { 1 2 3 4 5 6 } MAT TRANSP")) = "3x2 { { 1 4 } { 2 5 } { 3 6 } }", "AND MATRIX WORDS ANSWER A MATRIX, NEVER AN ARRAY")

    Print("--- seed-money: exact, and refuses rather than losing a penny ---")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "5 MONEY 0 MSPLIT", "MSPLIT needs a whole number of shares of 1 or more, got 0"), "MSPLIT INTO ZERO SHARES")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1 5 MONEY MADD", "MADD needs two MONEYs, got NUMBER"), "MADD GIVEN A BARE NUMBER")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "10 MONEY 3 MSPLIT")) = "{ $3.34 $3.33 $3.33 }", "AND MSPLIT LOSES NO PENNY")

    Print("--- seed-seq: FIRST, REST, SORTL ---")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ } FIRST", "FIRST needs a non-empty list"), "FIRST OF NOTHING")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ } REST", "REST needs a non-empty list"), "REST OF NOTHING")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 1 " & Q("a") & " } SORTL", "SORTL needs every element to be the same kind"), "SORTL OF MIXED KINDS")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ TRUE FALSE } SORTL", "SORTL needs a list of NUMBERs or a list of STRINGs, got BOOLEAN"), "SORTL OF A KIND WITH NO ORDER")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "{ 3 1 2 } SORTL")) = "{ 1 2 3 }", "AND SORTL STILL SORTS")

    Print("--- seed-str: SPLIT, TOFIXED ---")
    Assert(Refuses(g, Q("a b") & " " & Q("") & " SPLIT", "SPLIT needs a separator that is not empty"), "SPLIT ON AN EMPTY SEPARATOR")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1.5 16 TOFIXED", "TOFIXED needs a whole number of places no wider than 15"), "TOFIXED PAST FIFTEEN PLACES")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1.5 2.5 TOFIXED", "TOFIXED needs a whole number of places no wider than 15"), "AND A FRACTIONAL PLACE COUNT")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "3.14159 2 TOFIXED")) = Q("3.14"), "AND TOFIXED STILL WORKS")

    Print("--- seed-dict: DHAS, DGET, DPUT, DKEYS ---")
    Let d1 = Ran(s0, Q("rate") & " 0.05 PAIR 1 GATHER")
    Assert(Text(d1) = "{ ( " & Q("rate") & " 0.05 ) }", "PAIR AND GATHER MAKE A ONE-ENTRY DICT")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ } " & Q("rate") & " DGET", "DGET found no entry for that key"), "DGET ON A KEY THAT IS NOT THERE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(d1, Q("rate") & " DGET")) = "0.05", "AND DGET FINDS IT WHEN IT IS")
    Assert(Text(Ran(d1, Q("cap") & " 100 DPUT")) = "{ ( " & Q("rate") & " 0.05 ) ( " & Q("cap") & " 100 ) }", "DPUT GROWS THE DICT, APPENDING A NEW KEY AT THE END")

    Print("--- seed-random: guarded reproducibly under SEED ---")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "5 1 RANDOMRANGE", "RANDOMRANGE needs hi at or above lo, got 5 and 1"), "RANDOMRANGE WITH HI BELOW LO")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "5 1 RANDOMINT", "RANDOMINT needs hi at or above lo, got 5 and 1"), "RANDOMINT WITH HI BELOW LO")
    Let r1 = Ran(s0, "42 SEED 1 6 RANDOMINT")
    Let r2 = Ran(s0, "42 SEED 1 6 RANDOMINT")
    Assert(Text(r1) = Text(r2), "SEED MAKES THE SESSION REPRODUCIBLE")

    Print("--- seed-clock: total, and FORMATDURATION alone touches nothing ---")
    Assert(Holds(RckHelp(s0, "TICKS"), "touches the world"), "READING THE CLOCK IS AN EFFECT")
    Assert(Holds(RckHelp(s0, "FORMATDURATION"), "touches nothing"), "FORMATTING A NUMBER ALREADY ON THE STACK IS NOT")
    Assert(Deep(Ran(s0, "STAMPDATE")) = 1, "STAMPDATE ANSWERS ONE CELL")

    Print("--- seed-bool: every refusal bool.shoddy would otherwise abort on ---")
    Rem bool is the strictest machine in the tree - it refuses a negative,
    Rem a fraction, a value that does not fit its width and a width outside
    Rem 1..53 - and every one of those refusals is an Error. This seed's
    Rem whole job is asking each question first and answering instead, so
    Rem the guard list below IS the seed's reason for existing.
    Assert(Refuses(g, "0 1 - 1 BITAND", "BITAND needs a whole number of 0 or more, got -1"), "A NEGATIVE BIT PATTERN")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1.5 0 BITAT", "BITAT needs a whole number of 0 or more, got 1.5"), "AND A FRACTIONAL ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "2 53 ^ POPCOUNT", "POPCOUNT needs a whole number below 2^53"), "AND ONE PAST THE 53-BIT CEILING, WHERE IT IS NO LONGER EXACTLY ITSELF")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "256 8 BITNOT", "BITNOT needs a value that fits 8 bits, and 256 does not"), "A VALUE TOO WIDE FOR ITS WIDTH")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "0 54 BITNOT", "BITNOT needs a width from 1 to 53, got 54"), "A WIDTH ABOVE THE CEILING")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "0 0 BITNOT", "BITNOT needs a width from 1 to 53, got 0"), "AND ONE BELOW ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "5 37 INBASE", "INBASE needs a base from 2 to 36, got 37"), "A BASE THE DIGIT ALPHABET CANNOT SPELL")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "5 1 INBASE", "INBASE needs a base from 2 to 36, got 1"), "AND A BASE OF ONE")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "0 53 BITAT", "BITAT needs a bit index from 0 to 52, got 53"), "A BIT INDEX PAST THE LAST ONE A 53-BIT WORD HAS")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "54 MASK", "MASK needs a bit count from 0 to 53, got 54"), "A MASK WIDER THAN THE CEILING")
    Rem SHL reads the session width now and takes two arguments, not
    Rem three. The shift is still bounded and still named.
    Assert(Refuses(g, "1 99 SHL", "SHL needs a shift from 0 to 53, got 99"), "A SHIFT PAST THE CEILING")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "255 54 SHR", "SHR needs a shift from 0 to 53, got 54"), "AND THE SAME FOR THE ONE SHIFT THAT TAKES NO WIDTH")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "171 4 7 GETFIELD", "GETFIELD needs a high bit at or above the low bit, got 4 and 7"), "AN INVERTED FIELD")
    Rem FROMBASE reads text, so bool answers Result rather than aborting;
    Rem this is the one guard the seed translates rather than invents, and
    Rem the message it hands on names FROMBASE, not BOOLFROMBASE.
    Assert(Refuses(g, Q("1F") & " 2 FROMBASE", "FROMBASE: '1F' HAS NO DIGIT F IN BASE 2 at position 2"), "A DIGIT THE BASE DOES NOT HAVE, WITH ITS 1-BASED POSITION")
    Assert(Refuses(g, Q("") & " 16 FROMBASE", "FROMBASE: THE EMPTY STRING IS NOT A NUMBER IN BASE 16"), "AND AN EMPTY STRING, WHICH HAS NO POSITION TO REPORT")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "TRUE 1 XOR", "XOR needs a BOOLEAN, got NUMBER"), "A GATE HANDED A NUMBER")
    Rem The families that arrived with the session width bring one new
    Rem KIND of refusal between them and not thirty: a value that does not
    Rem fit the width the session is set to. One from each family is
    Rem walked here; tst/seedbooltest.shoddy walks every word of all of
    Rem them, which is what a per-seed suite is for and this file is not.
    Assert(Refuses(Ran(g, "8 WIDTH"), "256 1 ROTL", "ROTL needs a value that fits 8 bits, and 256 does not"), "A ROTATE OF A VALUE TOO WIDE FOR THE SESSION")
    Assert(Refuses(Ran(g, "8 WIDTH"), "256 1 ADDW", "ADDW needs a value that fits 8 bits, and 256 does not"), "AND THE SAME IN THE WRAPPING ARITHMETIC")
    Assert(Refuses(Ran(g, "8 WIDTH"), "128 UNSIGNED", "UNSIGNED needs a value from -128 to 127 at 8 signed bits"), "AND A SIGNED VALUE OUTSIDE WHAT THE WIDTH HOLDS, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT BOUND AND SAYS SO")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "10 FROMBCD", "HAS THE NIBBLE 10, WHICH IS NOT A DECIMAL DIGIT"), "A BCD NIBBLE THAT IS NOT A DECIMAL DIGIT, TRANSLATED FROM BOOL'S RESULT RATHER THAN INVENTED")
    Assert(Refuses(g, "{ 0 1 } 13 MINTERMS", "MINTERMS needs a variable count from 1 to 12, got 13"), "AND A TRUTH TABLE BIG ENOUGH TO HANG, REFUSED BY NAME INSTEAD")
    Rem LOWBIT is the one that does NOT refuse where a caller might expect
    Rem it to: zero has no lowest set bit and that is an ordinary answer,
    Rem so it answers an empty LIST. Asserted here as an answer, beside the
    Rem refusals, because the distinction is the design.
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "0 LOWBIT")) = "{ }", "AND ZERO'S LOWEST BIT IS AN ABSENCE ANSWERED, NOT A REFUSAL")

    Print("--- seed-bool: and the answers ---")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "255 HEX")) = Q("FF"), "HEX")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "5 BIN")) = Q("101"), "BIN")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "1295 36 INBASE")) = Q("ZZ"), "INBASE AT THE TOP OF THE ALPHABET")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, Q("ff") & " 16 FROMBASE")) = "255", "FROMBASE, CASE-INSENSITIVE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "255 HEX 16 FROMBASE")) = "255", "AND THE ROUND TRIP THROUGH BOTH")
    Rem BITAND, BITOR and BITXOR are the three width-free words, which is
    Rem why none of them takes a width here either.
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "12 10 BITAND")) = "8", "BITAND")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "12 10 BITOR")) = "14", "BITOR")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "12 10 BITXOR")) = "6", "BITXOR")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "0 8 BITNOT")) = "255", "BITNOT WITHIN A BYTE")
    Rem The width is a session mode, so it is set once at the front of the
    Rem line rather than carried by every shift on it. At the default 32
    Rem the second of these would be 256; the whole point of WIDTH is that
    Rem the bit falls off where the session says the word ends.
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "8 WIDTH 1 7 SHL")) = "128", "SHL TO THE TOP OF A BYTE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "8 WIDTH 1 8 SHL")) = "0", "AND ONE PLACE FURTHER FALLS OFF")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "1 8 SHL")) = "256", "WHILE AT THE DEFAULT WIDTH IT IS STILL THERE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "255 4 SHR")) = "15", "SHR")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "8 MASK")) = "255", "MASK")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "171 7 4 GETFIELD")) = "10", "GETFIELD READS THE HIGH NIBBLE OF 171")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "12 2 BITAT")) = "True", "BITAT ANSWERS A BOOLEAN, NOT A ONE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "12 0 BITAT")) = "False", "AND A FALSE, NOT A ZERO")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "255 POPCOUNT")) = "8", "POPCOUNT")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "TRUE TRUE NAND")) = "False", "NAND")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "FALSE FALSE NOR")) = "True", "NOR")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "TRUE FALSE XOR")) = "True", "XOR")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "TRUE TRUE XNOR")) = "True", "XNOR")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "TRUE FALSE IMPLIES")) = "False", "IMPLIES, ON ITS ONE FALSE ROW")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s0, "FALSE FALSE IFF")) = "True", "IFF")
    Rem The same rule seed-math applied to Rad and Deg: the engine core
    Rem already owns these three, so seed-bool does not redefine them.
    Assert(SeedOf(s0, "AND") = "core", "AND IS STILL THE ENGINE CORE'S, NOT SEED-BOOL'S")
    Assert(SeedOf(s0, "NOT") = "core", "AND SO IS NOT")
    Assert(SeedOf(s0, "XOR") = "seed-bool", "WHILE XOR, WHICH THE CORE LACKS, IS SEED-BOOL'S")

    Print("--- T15: abort-safety across real I/O, survival proved by the next line ---")
    Assert(Refuses(g, Q("tst/csv/does-not-exist.txt") & " READLINES", "CANNOT READ"), "READLINES ON A MISSING PATH")
    Assert(Refuses(g, Q("tst/csv") & " READLINES", "CANNOT READ"), "READLINES ON A DIRECTORY")
    Assert(Survives(g, Q("tst/csv/no/such/dir/out.txt") & " { " & Q("a") & " } WRITELINES"), "WRITELINES TO A PATH WHOSE DIRECTORY DOES NOT EXIST")
    Assert(Text(Ran(g, Q("tst/csv/no/such/dir/out.txt") & " { " & Q("a") & " } WRITELINES")) = "False | 2 | 1", "AND ANSWERS FALSE RATHER THAN AN ERROR")
    Assert(Refuses(g, Q("tst/csv/does-not-exist.csv") & " CSVLOAD", "CANNOT READ"), "CSVLOAD ON A MISSING PATH")
    Assert(Refuses(g, Q("tst/csv/malformed.csv") & " CSVLOAD", "found malformed CSV"), "CSVLOAD ON A MALFORMED DOCUMENT")
    Assert(Survives(g, "999 " & Q("nope") & " ISAMGETOR"), "ISAMGETOR ON A MISSING KEY")
    Assert(Text(Ran(g, "999 " & Q("nope") & " ISAMGETOR")) = Q("nope") & " | 2 | 1", "AND ANSWERS THE DEFAULT UNCHANGED, RATHER THAN A REFUSAL")

    Print("--- T16: no handle ever reaches the stack ---")
    Assert(All(RckDict(s0), Fn(e) => Instr(Lower(WordEffect(e)), "handle") = 0), "NO WORD'S EFFECT STRING MENTIONS A HANDLE")
    Assert(Bad(s0, "1 BOPEN"), "BOPEN IS AN UNKNOWN WORD")
    Assert(Why(s0, "1 BOPEN") = "unknown word BOPEN", "NAMING IT")
    Assert(Bad(s0, Q("x") & " 16 ISAMOPEN"), "ISAMOPEN TOO")
    Assert(Bad(s0, "1 2 SEEK"), "AND SEEK")
    Rem A hundred loads of the same file leaks nothing, since CSVLOAD opens,
    Rem reads and closes inside TRYREADFILE every time — there is no handle
    Rem on the stack for UNDO to strand or for a hundred calls to exhaust.
    Let loaded100 = Fold(Range(1, 100), s0, Fn(a, k) => Ran(a, Q(SalesPath()) & " CSVLOAD DROP"))
    Assert(Deep(loaded100) = 0, "A HUNDRED LOADS OF THE SAME FILE SUCCEED AND LEAVE NOTHING BEHIND")

    Print("--- T17: the data session ---")
    Let s1 = Ran(s0, Q(SalesPath()) & " CSVLOAD " & Q("S") & " STO")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s1, Q("S") & " RCL CSVWIDTH")) = "4", "THE FIXTURE IS FOUR COLUMNS WIDE")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s1, Q("S") & " RCL LENGTH")) = "251", "AND TWO HUNDRED FIFTY ROWS PLUS ITS HEADER")
    Let s2 = Ran(s1, Q("S") & " RCL " & Q("Amount") & " CSVCOLUMNNUMS " & Q("C") & " STO")
    Assert(Deep(Ran(s2, Q("C") & " RCL")) = 1, "THE COLUMN IS ONE CELL")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s2, Q("C") & " RCL LENGTH")) = "250", "OF TWO HUNDRED FIFTY NUMBERS")
    Rem The independent half of T17.3: stats.shoddy called directly on the
    Rem same fixture, parsed by hand rather than through the engine.
    Let amounts = SalesAmounts()
    Assert(Near(TopNum(Ran(s2, Q("C") & " RCL MEAN")), Mean(amounts)), "MEAN AGREES WITH STATS.SHODDY CALLED DIRECTLY")
    Assert(Near(TopNum(Ran(s2, Q("C") & " RCL STDDEV")), StdDev(amounts)), "AND SO DOES STDDEV")
    Assert(Near(TopNum(Ran(s2, Q("C") & " RCL 0.95 QUANTILE")), Quantile(amounts, 0.95)), "AND QUANTILE AT 0.95")
    Rem CSVPAIRS zips a row against the header; DGET reads it back exactly
    Rem as seed-dict's own association list would.
    Let firstRow = Ran(s2, Q("S") & " RCL " & Q("S") & " RCL CSVBODY FIRST CSVPAIRS")
    Assert(Text(Ran(firstRow, Q("Region") & " DGET")) = Q("South"), "CSVPAIRS THEN DGET READS THE FIRST ROW'S REGION")
    Assert(Text(Ran(firstRow, Q("Date") & " DGET")) = Q("2025-01-01"), "AND ITS DATE")
    Rem CSVGET stays strict (R5's note): a column the header does not
    Rem promise is worth hearing about, so it is refused rather than
    Rem defaulted — the one guard CSVCOLUMN/CSVCOLUMNNUMS trade away on
    Rem purpose, in favour of a scan that does not stop at a ragged row.
    Assert(Refuses(s1, Q("S") & " RCL " & Q("S") & " RCL CSVBODY FIRST " & Q("Nope") & " CSVGET", "CSVGET found no column named 'Nope'"), "CSVGET ON A COLUMN THE HEADER DOES NOT NAME")
    Rem CSVGET's other half: the header promises a column and the ROW stops
    Rem short of it. Built on the stack rather than from a fixture, since a
    Rem ragged document is two literals and needs no file.
    Assert(Refuses(s1, "{ { " & Q("a") & " " & Q("b") & " } { " & Q("1") & " } } { " & Q("1") & " } " & Q("b") & " CSVGET", "CSVGET: the row stops short of column 'b'"), "AND ON A ROW THAT STOPS SHORT OF ONE IT DOES NAME")
    Assert(Refuses(s1, Q("S") & " RCL " & Q("Nope") & " CSVCOLUMN", "CSVCOLUMN found no column named 'Nope'"), "AND CSVCOLUMN, THE SAME WAY")
    Assert(Refuses(s1, Q("S") & " RCL " & Q("Nope") & " CSVCOLUMNNUMS", "CSVCOLUMNNUMS found no column named 'Nope'"), "AND CSVCOLUMNNUMS")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s1, Q("S") & " RCL " & Q("S") & " RCL CSVBODY FIRST " & Q("Region") & " CSVGET")) = Q("South"), "AND CSVGET STILL WORKS ON A COLUMN THAT IS THERE")

    Rem A column is named by header OR by 1-based position, and the two
    Rem address the same column: Amount is the fixture's third. The
    Rem positional form is what the worked data session keys, so it is the
    Rem one the acceptance criterion runs on.
    Print("--- either spelling names a column, and both name the same one ---")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s1, Q("S") & " RCL 3 CSVCOLUMNNUMS " & Q("S") & " RCL " & Q("Amount") & " CSVCOLUMNNUMS =")) = "True", "3 CSVCOLUMNNUMS IS " & Q("AMOUNT") & " CSVCOLUMNNUMS")
    Assert(Text(Ran(s1, Q("S") & " RCL 1 CSVCOLUMN " & Q("S") & " RCL " & Q("Date") & " CSVCOLUMN =")) = "True", "AND 1 CSVCOLUMN IS THE DATE COLUMN")
    Rem Three refusals for the three ways a position can be wrong, and one
    Rem for a cell that is neither spelling: 0 and 99 are off the end, 2.5
    Rem is a typo, and answering any of them would hide one.
    Assert(Refuses(s1, Q("S") & " RCL 0 CSVCOLUMNNUMS", "CSVCOLUMNNUMS needs a column from 1 to 4, got 0"), "A POSITION BELOW ONE IS REFUSED")
    Assert(Refuses(s1, Q("S") & " RCL 99 CSVCOLUMN", "CSVCOLUMN needs a column from 1 to 4, got 99"), "AND ONE PAST THE LAST COLUMN")
    Assert(Refuses(s1, Q("S") & " RCL 2.5 CSVCOLUMN", "CSVCOLUMN needs a column from 1 to 4, got 2.5"), "AND ONE THAT IS NOT WHOLE")
    Assert(Refuses(s1, Q("S") & " RCL TRUE CSVCOLUMN", "CSVCOLUMN needs a STRING header or a NUMBER column, got BOOLEAN"), "AND A CELL THAT IS NEITHER SPELLING")

    Print("--- Appendix A.3, the golden session, entered exactly as written ---")
    Rem The appendix's own lines, positional column and all. Its printed
    Rem figures are illustrative of a file this repository does not ship;
    Rem the numbers here are asserted against stats.shoddy called directly
    Rem on the committed fixture, which is the stronger check anyway.
    Let a1 = Ran(s0, Q(SalesPath()) & " CSVLOAD " & Q("S") & " STO")
    Assert(Text(Ran(a1, Q("S") & " RCL CSVWIDTH " & Q("S") & " RCL LENGTH")) = "251 | 4", "y: 4, x: 251")
    Let a2 = Ran(a1, Q("S") & " RCL 3 CSVCOLUMNNUMS " & Q("C") & " STO")
    Assert(Near(TopNum(Ran(a2, Q("C") & " RCL MEAN")), Mean(amounts)), "y: THE MEAN")
    Assert(Near(TopNum(Ran(a2, Q("C") & " RCL STDDEV")), StdDev(amounts)), "x: THE STANDARD DEVIATION")
    Assert(Near(TopNum(Ran(a2, Q("C") & " RCL 0.95 QUANTILE")), Quantile(amounts, 0.95)), "AND THE 95TH PERCENTILE")
    Rem The last line is the contract, not the feature (Appendix A.3): a
    Rem mistyped path answers an error value and the stack — the register,
    Rem the dictionary, the session — is exactly as it was.
    Let a3 = Ran(a2, Q("C") & " RCL")
    Assert(Bad(a3, Q("tst/csv/missing.csv") & " CSVLOAD"), "A MISTYPED PATH IS REFUSED")
    Assert(Text(Ran(a3, Q("tst/csv/missing.csv") & " CSVLOAD")) = Text(a3), "AND THE STACK IS EXACTLY AS IT WAS")
    Assert(Text(Ran(Ran(a3, Q("tst/csv/missing.csv") & " CSVLOAD"), "LENGTH")) = "250", "THE COLUMN IS STILL BANKED, AND THE SESSION CONTINUES")

    Rem R2.9 at its shortest, and the acceptance criterion verbatim: load a
    Rem delimited file, take a column, describe it — one line, from the
    Rem entry line, with the path the only thing changed from the criterion
    Rem as written. This is the assertion that separates a dictionary that
    Rem can open a file from a pocket calculator.
    Print("--- data in, answer out, in one line ---")
    Let one = Ran(s0, Q(SalesPath()) & " CSVLOAD  3 CSVCOLUMNNUMS  MEAN")
    Assert(Deep(one) = 1, "THE WHOLE SESSION LEAVES ONE CELL")
    Assert(Near(TopNum(one), Mean(amounts)), "AND IT IS THE MEAN OF THE AMOUNT COLUMN")

    Print("")
    Print("ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED")

Rem ---- one more shared shape ------------------------------------------

Def Holds(lines As List Of String, sub As String) As Boolean
    Any(lines, Fn(l) => Instr(l, sub) > 0)

Def SeedOf(st As RckState, name As String) As String
    Select Case RckFind(st, name)
        Case Some(e)
            WordSeed(e)
        Case Else
            "NOT A WORD"
